ectrons  are moving on to this  carbon atom in this resonance form now  when we push our electrons this way we  get a tertiary carbo cation this ch3 can  actually donate electron density through  an effect called hyper conjugation and  that's what makes the tertiary carbo  cation more stable so this is a much  better resonance form and we shouldn't  even consider this one so now we're  gonna draw more resonance forms for this  we're gonna just push two electron pairs  at a time so we're gonna push this one  and this one and that's how I tell my  students to start out when you're  writing resonance forms don't try to  push through a whole conjugated system  if you're not seeing it yet if you push  too if you make two arrows push two  pairs of electrons you'll be able to see  each resonance form that's contributing  so let's try that this lone pair pushes  in this one we don't want to make five  bonds to carbon right here so this  electron pair goes up onto that carbon  and let's draw what we get and in our  final resonance form that will draw  we're gonna push these in push this  double bond up onto this carbon so that  we don't make five bonds to that carbon  right there now although these resonance  forms don't contribute as much as this  one that's not charge separated they do  contribute and so the most electron rich  positions are here here and here  so from this analysis we can say that  alkyl groups are ortho-para directors  but since carbon doesn't have a lone  pair on it that it can push directly  into the ring or anything like that they  are weak activators of the Ring the next  group we're going to look at is the  halogens here we have bromobenzene we  really couldn't discuss this in the last  video because we were looking for  substitution where this was bonded to  something else and we use that to  evaluate whether or not it would be an  electron withdrawing group or an  electron donating group but the bromine  isn't bonded to anything else so what do  w